---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import FurtherReading from '../../components/FurtherReading.astro';
import SeaBreak from '../../components/SeaBreak.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import CTABand from '../../components/CTABand.astro';
import Layers from '../../components/Layers.astro';
import { celestial, ocean } from '../../config/images';
const crumbs = [
  { label: 'Explore Astrology', href: '/explore/' },
  { label: 'Schools & Traditions', href: '/explore/schools/' },
];
const schools = [
  ['Hellenistic', 'Planetary condition, sect, houses, lots, and timing techniques.'],
  ['Medieval & Renaissance', 'Planetary condition, rulership, prediction, elections, and horary.'],
  ['Modern psychological', 'Character, meaning, development, and the inner life.'],
  ['Evolutionary', 'Pluto, the lunar nodes, and a developmental or spiritual framework.'],
  ['Jyotish', 'A distinct Indian tradition with its own texts, techniques, timing systems, and philosophical context.'],
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Schools and Traditions in Astrology"
  description="Hellenistic, medieval, modern psychological, evolutionary, and Vedic astrology: how major approaches differ, where techniques come from, and why many astrologers study across traditions."
  path="/explore/schools/"
  breadcrumbs={crumbs}
>
  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={crumbs} />
  <section class="hero hero--split">
    <div class="container">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Explore Astrology</p>
        <h1>Schools and traditions</h1>
        <p class="lede">Astrology is not one single method. It is a long conversation made up of different traditions, techniques, assumptions, and ways of interpreting a chart. Knowing some of that history helps you understand why two thoughtful astrologers may not work the same way.</p>
      </div>
      <img class="hero__img" src={ocean.meetingWaves.src} srcset={ocean.meetingWaves.srcset} sizes="100vw" alt={ocean.meetingWaves.alt} width={ocean.meetingWaves.width} height={ocean.meetingWaves.height} fetchpriority="high" />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container prose flow">
      <h2>Why this matters to you</h2>
      <p>
        Different schools may use different house systems, timing methods, planets, philosophical
        assumptions, and rules for judging planetary condition. Those choices can change what an
        astrologer notices and how a reading is framed. The labels below are orientation points,
        not complete definitions.
      </p>
      <div class="school-grid">
        {schools.map(([name, emphasis]) => (
          <article>
            <h3>{name}</h3>
            <p>{emphasis}</p>
          </article>
        ))}
      </div>

      <h2>What to ask an astrologer</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>What have you studied, and which techniques do you use most often?</li>
        <li>How do you decide which techniques matter for a particular question?</li>
        <li>Which house system or systems do you use, and why?</li>
        <li>How do you approach prediction, uncertainty, and client choice?</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The value of these questions is not that there is one correct answer. It is that the answer
        tells you how the astrologer thinks. A practitioner should be able to explain the methods
        they use without asking you to accept a label as proof of expertise. More in
        <a href="/blog/how-to-choose-an-astrologer/">how to choose an astrologer</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Hellenistic astrology <span class="card__meta">roughly 1st century BCE–7th century CE</span></h2>
      <p>
        Much of the technical foundation of Western astrology developed in the Hellenistic world,
        especially in Greco-Roman Egypt. Surviving texts describe the seven visible planets, whole
        sign houses, <a href="/explore/dignities/">essential dignities</a>, sect, lots, and a range
        of timing techniques. Modern astrologers have regained access to much of this material
        through translation and scholarship over the last several decades.
      </p>

      <h2>Medieval and Renaissance astrology</h2>
      <p>
        Astrological knowledge moved through Persian and Arabic scholars and later into Latin
        Europe, where techniques continued to develop. This lineage includes extensive work with
        planetary condition, rulership, prediction, elections, and <strong>horary astrology</strong>—
        a branch that casts a chart for the moment a specific question is asked. My own study of
        horary is one part of my training, not a label for my entire practice.
      </p>

      <h2>Modern psychological astrology <span class="card__meta">20th century</span></h2>
      <p>
        Modern Western astrology increasingly emphasized character, meaning, development, and the
        inner life. It incorporated Uranus, Neptune, and Pluto and often drew from psychological
        language. Compared with many older approaches, it generally placed less emphasis on
        traditional dignity and concrete prediction and more on how a person experiences and
        develops the chart.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <SeaBreak image={celestial.paperBoat} />

  <section class="section section--sea">
    <div class="container prose flow">
      <h2>Evolutionary astrology</h2>
      <p>
        Evolutionary astrology developed in the late twentieth century and places particular
        emphasis on Pluto and the lunar nodes. Many practitioners use ideas such as soul development
        and reincarnation as part of the interpretive framework. Those premises may feel meaningful
        to some people and not to others, which is one reason it helps to know the framework being
        used.
      </p>

      <h2>Vedic (Jyotish) astrology</h2>
      <p>
        Jyotish is a distinct Indian astrological tradition with its own history, texts, techniques,
        timing systems, and philosophical context. It commonly uses a sidereal zodiac rather than
        the tropical zodiac used by most Western astrologers. It should not be treated as simply a
        different setting applied to an otherwise identical Western chart.
      </p>

      <Layers kind="mo">
        <p>
          I have studied across astrological traditions and methods, including horary, modern and
          contemporary approaches, Uranian techniques, dwarf planets, and older Western material.
          I am less interested in finding a label broad enough to contain all of that than in using
          the techniques that help me understand the chart and question in front of me. Study gives
          me tools. Synthesis is deciding which tools matter here, and conversation helps show how
          the pattern is actually being lived. <a href="/approach/">More on how I work.</a>
        </p>
      </Layers>
      <FurtherReading />
    </div>
  </section>

  <CTABand
    heading="Study widely. Read the chart in front of you."
    body="My practice draws from more than one astrological tradition, with technique serving synthesis rather than the other way around."
    buttonLabel="Read about Mo's approach"
    buttonHref="/approach/"
  />

  <style>
    .school-grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: var(--space-4); margin-block: var(--space-6); }
    .school-grid article { padding: var(--space-5); border: 1px solid var(--border-color, currentColor); border-radius: var(--radius-lg, 1rem); }
    .school-grid h3, .school-grid p { margin-bottom: 0; }
    @media (min-width: 768px) { .school-grid { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); } }
  </style>
</BaseLayout>
